+// thread is kept in a table keyed by pthread_self() instead.
+//
+// Only the owning thread reads or writes its entry, so a slot is claimed with one CAS and
+// read without one; reads are async-signal safe. Clearing (set_thread(NULL), done on every
+// thread exit path) leaves a tombstone that later inserts reuse, so the table stays bounded
+// by the peak number of live threads, not by how many have existed.
